.el .ds Aq '
.TH iron-vault-export 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-export \- Write the vault as a migration bundle to BUNDLE, for `import` on another machine. The service checks the vault, prompts for the vault passphrase to authorize the export (switchable to a `tpm` approver\*(Aqs TPM approval PIN), then for a new export passphrase, typed twice, which seals the bundle; `import` asks for it. The bundle arrives in `.<BUNDLE name>.partial` beside BUNDLE, created with mode 0600 before anything is sent, and takes BUNDLE\*(Aqs name only once it is whole. An existing BUNDLE or a leftover partial file is refused before anything is sent. The source vault keeps serving afterwards
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault export\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIBUNDLE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Write the vault as a migration bundle to BUNDLE, for `import` on another machine. The service checks the vault, prompts for the vault passphrase to authorize the export (switchable to a `tpm` approver\*(Aqs TPM approval PIN), then for a new export passphrase, typed twice, which seals the bundle; `import` asks for it. The bundle arrives in `.<BUNDLE name>.partial` beside BUNDLE, created with mode 0600 before anything is sent, and takes BUNDLE\*(Aqs name only once it is whole. An existing BUNDLE or a leftover partial file is refused before anything is sent. The source vault keeps serving afterwards
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIBUNDLE\fR>
Where to write the bundle; it must not exist
